.TH CBSTMAP 7 "2026-10-06" "c_collections" "C Collections Library - Overview"
.SH NAME
CBSTMAP \- overview of the cbstmap ordered map
.SH LIBRARY
C Collections (libccollections, \-lccollections)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <ccollections/cbstmap.h>
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.nf
A cbmap maps keys to values and keeps the keys in sorted order. It is a
self-balancing AVL tree, so an insert, a lookup and a delete each take
O(log n) time in the worst case, whatever the order of the inserts. An
iteration visits the entries in ascending key order; that order is part
of the contract (see cbmap_begin_iter(3)).

The key type selects the default order:
  - char: the native order of char.
  - The signed integer types: a signed order.
  - The unsigned integer types and pointers: an unsigned order.
  - The floating types: a numeric order, in which a NaN sorts above all
    other values.
  - A string: the strcmp order.
  - All other key types, for example a struct: a raw memcmp of the key
    bytes. A custom comparison procedure replaces this order.
cbmap_create_full(3) gives the complete rules, and cbmap_construct_cc(3)
gives the contract of a comparison procedure.

The API has two layers:
  - The type-inferred macros, for example cbmap_construct(3),
    cbmap_insert(3), cbmap_get(3), cbmap_get_ptr(3) and cbmap_remove(3).
    They keep the key type and the value type with the handle, and on a
    hard error they stop the program through ccol_fatal_err().
  - The raw functions: cbmap_create_full(3), cbmap_insert_elem(3),
    cbmap_get_elem_ref(3), cbmap_get_elem_copy(3) and
    cbmap_delete_elem(3). They take each key and each value as a
    cmap_pair and return a ccol_retval_t.
.fi
.SH "OWNERSHIP OF KEYS AND VALUES"
.nf
The map keeps its own copy of each key and each value: an insert copies
the bytes of the key and of the value into storage that the map owns,
and an update frees the old value.

A char * key or value is a NUL-terminated string. The map copies the
contents of the string, with the terminator, and the caller keeps its
own string, which it can change or free as soon as the insert returns.

Some calls give back a string pointer: cbmap_get(3), cbmap_get_ptr(3),
cbmap_get_elem_ref(3) and an iterator. That pointer points into the
storage of the map, so do not free it. It is valid until the next
insert, the next delete, a reset or the destroy of the map. To replace
a stored string, insert the key again.

A value of any other pointer type is kept as the pointer itself: the
map does not copy, follow or free the object at that address. The
caller must free such objects, for example by walking over the map and
freeing them immediately before cbmap_destroy(3).
.fi
.SH "THREAD SAFETY"
.nf
A map has no internal lock. A caller can share one map between threads,
but it must then serialize each call that changes the map against all
other calls on that map.
.fi
